Subject: [PATCH] 286/287: adjacency is closed, delta lost, and the real gap is
 the corpus split

287 rerun, controls now working: permutation null 0.4987 and 0.4997 at
|z| < 0.05, redundant-channel null 0.4993. The estimator can say no, so its
no means something. SECOND_LAYER_REDUNDANT on both spans - U|L reads 0.494
(z -0.22) at span 2 and 0.467 (z -1.17) at span 3, and order_content is
0.0129 and 0.0134. Destroying word order entirely moves the candidate upper
ink by just over one percent, and none of that percent is unique. Adjacency
is closed as a route to a second layer, cleanly and with a working examiner.

--ctx-channel delta, same tapes as the plain relational arm. My prediction
was that the replacement arm was the one the evidence pointed at. It lost.

  matched @votes   plain 0.431 vs 0.431 tie   delta 0.414 vs 0.431 LOSS
  lie @votes       plain 0.487 vs 0.462 win   delta 0.359 vs 0.462 LOSS
  dup x3 accuracy  plain 0.773                delta 0.531
  verdict          EVIDENCE_PARTIAL           EVIDENCE_NO

So the composition residual is not merely redundant, substituting it for the
plain cosine costs the lie win and a third of the duplication advantage. That
is three arms now - ink-slices, delta, and 287's spans - saying the same
thing from three constructions: at this tape size nothing further derived
from fp pays, and the plain relational mind is the best arm there is.

Which makes the open gates worth reading again, because they are no longer
about channels. The same delta arm on the same weights:

  unknown margin AUC   0.865 (z +5.59) on the training tape  ->  0.495 held out
  contested accuracy   0.889 on nine answered                ->  0.125 on eight

The mind learns honesty and contested weighing. It learns them well. They do
not cross the corpus split. Two explanations fit and nothing distinguishes
them: the 70/30 line split builds differently shaped addresses on the two
sides, or the mind does not generalise at all.

--holdout address separates them. It splits the ADDRESSES of one corpus by a
stable hash of the anchor rather than splitting the lines, so both sides are
built from the same text by the same process and the only thing new at
evaluation is the subject. Keyed on the anchor and not the address name,
because the fp group number in front of it is assigned by corpus order and
changes on every resample - a name is not stable across tapes, a subject is.
Verified: every relation of one subject lands on one side, and the split
comes out 0.503 over four thousand synthetic anchors.

Transfer under address holdout but not under corpus holdout blames the tape,
and the next move is on the write side where this project already says
improvement comes from. Failure under both blames the mind, and no further
input channel will fix it. Either way the question stops being open.

The cheap probe that costs no code at all should run first: --tape-period 50
gives 120 distinct training tapes instead of 12 at the same step count, and
the relational arm takes minutes. If the gap closes there it was never
distribution shift, only starvation.

